    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d289">

                <pb facs="0398" n="330" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v01/pg330"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72111/144: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        British Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Spring
                            Rice</hi>)<anchor n="1a" xml:id="d289-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-09-09T13:04:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 9, 1914—1:04 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Replying to your note of the 4th instant<anchor n="1b" xml:id="d289-f2"/> it
                    seems unnecessary to answer the inquiry regarding the shipment of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Merion’s</hi> guns to England as cargo in another
                    vessel, for the reason that Department is informed that notwithstanding the
                    assurances of your Government the <hi rendition="#italic">Merion</hi> sailed
                    with her guns and ammunition.<anchor n="1c" xml:id="d289-f3"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">W. J. Bryan</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d289-f1" n="1a" type="source" xml:id="d289-f1-content">
                <p>This telegram bears the notation: “In view of objection raised by British Amb.
                    that the last phrase reflected on British Govt. this telegram was withdrawn.
                    9/9/14 6 pm Robert Lansing.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d289-f2" n="1b" xml:id="d289-f2-content">
                <p>
                    <ref target="frus1914Supp#pg_606">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1914, supp., p.
                    606</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d289-f3" n="1c" xml:id="d289-f3-content">
                <p>For correspondence previously printed concerning the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Merion</hi>, see <ref target="frus1914Supp#pg_605">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, pp. 605</ref>, <ref
                        target="frus1914Supp#pg_606">606</ref>–<ref target="frus1914Supp#pg_607"
                        >607</ref>, <ref target="frus1914Supp#pg_612">612</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
